[10] what a 9/4-type claim would need (we do NOT attempt one)
  target omega = 2.25.  Under the <2,2,2> normalisation the bound is omega = log2(zeta),
  so omega = 2.25 requires zeta = 2^2.25 = 4.756828 instead of zeta = 7.
  Equivalently R_tau must be about 4.756828^tau rather than 7^tau,
  a saving factor of (4.756828/7)^tau = 0.679547^tau, i.e. -0.557355 bits per tensor power.
    tau= 4: 7^tau =                    2,401   needed ~ 2^9 =                      512   ratio 4.69x
    tau= 8: 7^tau =                5,764,801   needed ~ 2^18 =                  262,144   ratio 21.99x
    tau=16: 7^tau =       33,232,930,569,601   needed ~ 2^36 =           68,719,476,736   ratio 483.60x
  A checkable claim of this shape would have to supply ALL of:
    (a) an explicit finite decomposition of some <n,m,p> (or a tau-power), with
        integer/rational entries and its exact rank -- checkable by tensor_verify.py;
    (b) an argument that R_tau < R^tau genuinely holds (a better base decomposition,
        or a rectangular/disjoint combination that provably lowers the value);
    (c) the theory step turning that saving into an asymptotic-rank bound --
        laser method / asymptotic spectrum / support functionals. This is a
        mathematical proof, NOT a finite computation, and no decomposition
        verifier can check it.  That is the boundary of what this artifact does.
  The plain Kronecker ladder verified in strassen.py shows R_tau = 7^tau exactly,
  so no saving of this kind is present in Strassen's decomposition itself.

